# 8A. KNOWLEDGE BASE (built-in content stored locally as JSON in /data/knowledge, available offline, written in English, Hindi, and Kannada)

## 8A.1 Bleeding first aid (each step is one big card)
Opening line: "Call 108 or 112 now if the bleeding is heavy, spurting, or won't stop."
1. Stay safe. If possible, put on gloves or cover your hands with a clean plastic bag. Avoid touching blood with bare hands
2. Press directly and firmly on the wound with a clean cloth, gauze, or the cleanest material available. Keep pressing without lifting to look
3. If blood soaks through, do NOT remove the cloth. Add more cloth on top and keep pressing
4. Lay the person down. If there is no suspected head, neck, spine, or leg injury, raise their legs about 30 cm to help blood flow to the vital organs. Keep them warm with a blanket or coat
5. If something is stuck in the wound, do NOT pull it out. Pad around it and press around the object
6. For a limb with life-threatening bleeding that does not stop with firm pressure: apply a tourniquet (or a wide strip of strong cloth tied tightly with a stick to twist) about 5-7 cm above the wound, never over a joint. Tighten until the bleeding stops, write down the time, do not loosen it, and leave it for the ambulance crew. A tourniquet is painful, and that is expected
7. Do not give food or drink to a seriously injured person. Talk to them and keep them calm. Watch for shock: pale, cold, sweaty skin, fast weak pulse, confusion, drowsiness. If they become unresponsive and are not breathing normally, tell the user to follow the emergency operator's CPR instructions
8. Do not apply powders, turmeric, ash, oil, or other home remedies on a bleeding wound
Special cases (extra cards): nosebleed (sit up, lean forward, pinch the soft part of the nose for 10-15 minutes, do not tilt the head back); cut finger or small wound (rinse with clean water, press, cover with a clean bandage); amputated body part (wrap in clean damp cloth, place in a sealed bag, keep the bag on ice but not touching ice directly, send with the patient); head injury or suspected spine injury (do not move the person unless in danger); chest or abdominal wound (do not push exposed organs back in, cover loosely with a clean damp cloth, press around not on); heavy bleeding in pregnancy or after delivery (call 108 immediately, lay down on the left side)

## 8A.2 Who can receive whose blood (ABO and Rh compatibility)
Red blood cells (patient group: can receive from):
O-: O- | O+: O+, O- | A-: A-, O- | A+: A+, A-, O+, O- | B-: B-, O- | B+: B+, B-, O+, O- | AB-: AB-, A-, B-, O- | AB+: all groups
Donates red cells to (donor group: can give to):
O-: all | O+: O+, A+, B+, AB+ | A-: A-, A+, AB-, AB+ | A+: A+, AB+ | B-: B-, B+, AB-, AB+ | B+: B+, AB+ | AB-: AB-, AB+ | AB+: AB+ only
Plasma works in reverse: AB plasma can go to anyone, A plasma to A and O patients, B plasma to B and O patients, O plasma only to O patients
Platelets: ideally ABO-matched, but not always required; Rh-negative girls and women of child-bearing age should receive Rh-negative platelets
Whole blood is normally given only to the same group
Emergency note to display: "O-negative red cells are the emergency choice when the group is unknown, but hospitals follow their own protocol and O-negative is scarce, so request it early. Only doctors decide."
Rare groups exist (for example Bombay phenotype, Rh-null). A Bombay-phenotype patient can receive blood only from another Bombay-phenotype donor. Banks can flag rare blood in the app
Explain simply: a person with A antigens makes anti-B antibodies, B makes anti-A, AB makes neither, O makes both, and the Rh factor (+ or -) is another antigen. Mismatching causes a dangerous reaction

## 8A.3 How to find a blood group
- The reliable way is a lab test at a hospital, blood bank, or diagnostic lab: ABO and Rh typing (forward and reverse grouping). It is fast, usually costs very little, and gives a report
- Donating at a blood camp is another way to find out, since donors are tested and told their group
- Old records such as a hospital discharge card, a birth record, or a blood donor card can also show it, but should be re-confirmed if used for a decision
- Home test cards or slide kits exist, but they are for learning only and can give wrong results. NEVER use them to decide a transfusion
- Before any transfusion the hospital always re-tests the patient's group and does a cross-match, even when a group is already known. This prevents deadly reactions
- In the app: the user can enter their group in their profile and mark it "self-reported" or "lab-confirmed" (a bank can confirm it)

## 8A.4 Donation FAQs (short answers)
- Who can donate: healthy adults aged 18-65, weight at least 45 kg, haemoglobin at least 12.5 g/dL (configurable rules; verify with current national guidelines)
- How often: whole blood every 3 months for men and every 4 months for women (configurable)
- How long: whole-blood donation takes about 10-15 minutes, the whole visit about 45 minutes. Typically 350-450 mL is taken
- Before: eat a normal meal, drink water, sleep well, bring an ID, avoid alcohol for 24 hours
- After: rest 10-15 minutes, drink extra fluids, eat a snack, avoid heavy exercise and long driving for the rest of the day, keep the bandage on for several hours
- Temporary deferral: fever or infection, recent tattoo or piercing, recent surgery, pregnancy or breastfeeding, some medicines or vaccines, low haemoglobin. The blood bank doctor decides
- Myths: donating does not weaken you, the body replaces the fluid within hours and red cells within weeks; you cannot get a disease from donating because needles are sterile and single-use
- Components: one donation can be separated into red cells, plasma, and platelets and can help more than one patient
- Blood donation is voluntary and free of payment

## 8A.5 Emergency numbers
Ambulance 108, general emergency 112, blood helpline 104 (verify local numbers per state, editable by Admin)

